                <h2>本周三个明显变化</h2>
<p>本周从多个渠道了解到，Meta、TikTok等主流投放平台上，简单依赖AI生成的广告文案正在经历一轮“洗牌”：</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Meta广告质量评分收紧</strong>：平台对机械化模板化的AI文案识别更精准，被打上“低原创”标签的内容，曝光权重被系统性削弱。</li>
<li><strong>TikTok用户完播率下滑</strong>：用户对相似封面和相似开场白的识别能力明显提升，直接表现就是完播率和互动数据不好看。</li>
<li><strong>投放团队CTR普遍下降</strong>：多家出海团队的内部复盘显示，用AI快速生成的文案初稿，CTR比去年同期低了10%~20%，需要大量人工介入修改才能达到及格线。</li>
</ul>
<h2>三个直接影响</h2>
<p>这些变化带来的直接压力也很清晰：</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>用户审美疲劳加深</strong>。低价、爆款、限时抢这类关键词已经很难吸引注意力，用户对“一眼AI感”的内容会自动滑过。</li>
<li><strong>平台算法降权让成本上升</strong>。同样的预算，能拿到的自然流量变少了，CPM变相上涨。</li>
<li><strong>品牌辨识度被稀释</strong>。文案同质化不仅影响单条广告表现，长期来看用户记不住你是谁，品牌的溢价能力也会受损。</li>
</ol>
<h2>三条实操建议</h2>
<p>针对这些问题，本周给出海投放团队的三个调整方向：</p>
<p><strong>① 把AI定位从“直接输出”变成“框架搭建”</strong></p>
<p>让AI负责提供结构、变体和关键词组合，再由运营人员注入本地化洞察和情感元素。举个例子，某工具类App在东南亚市场投放时，AI生成了5个标题框架，运营在此基础上加入本地俚语和场景化描述，最终CTR比纯AI输出提升了22%。</p>
<p><strong>② 提高A/B测试频率</strong></p>
<p>AI可以快速批量生成多个版本，但哪个能跑出来得靠数据验证。建议本周开始，将文案素材的A/B测试周期从双周压缩到每周一次，重点观察标题前三秒的点击率和落地页跳出率两个指标。</p>
<p><strong>③ 建立文案素材库，形成“高转化特征库”</strong></p>
<p>把跑出高转化的文案特征提炼成模板：比如“痛点+数字+行动号召”的结构在某些品类表现更好，“场景故事+情绪共鸣”在另一些品类更有效。把这些特征喂给AI，让它生成的初稿更贴近高转化方向，避免每次从零开始。</p>
<hr />
<p>本周的变化是一个信号：AI是杠杆，用得好能放大创意，用不好就是在加速同质化。平台算法在变，用户口味在变，但核心始终是——你的文案有没有说用户想听的、有没有让用户记住。人工的判断和洞察，才是最终的竞争力。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Three Noticeable Changes This Week</h2>
<p>This week, sources across the industry indicate that ad copy simply relying on AI generation is undergoing a round of "cleansing" on major platforms like Meta and TikTok:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tighter Meta Ad Quality Scores</strong>: The platform has become more precise in identifying mechanical, templated AI copy. Content flagged as "low originality" faces systematic exposure weight reduction.</li>
<li><strong>Declining TikTok Completion Rates</strong>: Users' ability to recognize similar thumbnails and opening lines has notably improved, directly reflected in lower video completion rates and unfavorable engagement metrics.</li>
<li><strong>Universal CTR Decline for Teams</strong>: Internal reviews from multiple overseas teams show that AI-generated copy drafts have seen CTR drop 10%~20% compared to the same period last year, requiring substantial manual revisions to reach acceptable levels.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Three Direct Impacts</h2>
<p>The pressures brought by these changes are clear:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Deepening User Fatigue</strong>: Keywords like "low price," "bestseller," and "limited time deal" have become far less effective. Users automatically scroll past content with an "AI feel."</li>
<li><strong>Platform Algorithm Deprioritization Raising Costs</strong>: The same budget now yields less organic traffic, with CPMs effectively increasing.</li>
<li><strong>Diluted Brand Recognition</strong>: Homogenized copy affects not just individual ad performance but, over the long term, makes it harder for users to remember who you are, eroding brand premium.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Three Actionable Recommendations</h2>
<p>Here are three adjustment directions for overseas ad teams this week:</p>
<p><strong>① Reframe AI as "Framework Builder" Rather Than "Direct Output"</strong></p>
<p>Let AI handle structures, variations, and keyword combinations, then have operators inject localized insights and emotional elements. For example, a utility app targeting Southeast Asia had AI generate 5 title frameworks; the team added local slang and scenario-based descriptions, ultimately boosting CTR by 22% compared to pure AI output.</p>
<p><strong>② Increase A/B Testing Frequency</strong></p>
<p>AI can rapidly generate multiple versions, but data validation determines which ones perform. Starting this week, compress your copy A/B testing cycle from bi-weekly to weekly, focusing on click-through rate for the first three seconds of headlines and bounce rate on landing pages.</p>
<p><strong>③ Build a Copy Asset Library as a "High-Conversion Feature Database"</strong></p>
<p>Extract patterns from high-converting copy: for instance, the "pain point + number + call-to-action" structure performs better in certain categories, while "scenario story + emotional resonance" works better in others. Feed these features to AI so generated drafts align more closely with high-conversion directions, avoiding starting from scratch each time.</p>
<hr />
<p>This week's changes are a signal: AI is a lever—it amplifies creativity when used well and accelerates homogenization when used poorly. Platform algorithms change, user preferences shift, but the core remains: does your copy say what users want to hear? Does it make them remember? Human judgment and insight are the ultimate competitive edge.</p>
